An input-output pair with identical grids still carries information: the observed marker/object configuration did not activate a transformation. Log it alongside transformed examples, including its palette, geometry, marker colors, and surrounding background. Use it to distinguish broad rules (e.g. every sparse shape triggers extension) from conditional rules (only particular marker configurations or orientations do).

Before predicting an unchanged test grid, explicitly compare its structural features with all demonstrated positive and negative cases. If the demos establish a transformation for one motif but the test has a novel configuration, do not treat identity as a low-risk default; seek a discriminating demo when budget permits, or submit only a materially supported rule. Every demo response, including unchanged outputs, must update the trigger/scope ledger before another request or submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/unchanged-demo-trigger-check.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/repeated-pattern-misdirection.md", "content": "# Periodic output versus input-driven transformation

When several examples show periodic full-grid outputs, first verify whether the output is generated solely from a fixed palette cycle or whether it is a transformation of the input's marker arrangement. Record the complete mapping from each input row/cell to the corresponding output, including locations where the input is blank and output is nonblank. A small demo with distinctive sparse markers can reveal an operation such as filling a sequence, shifting or cycling colors, or extending a pattern; do not ignore it because other examples are dense stripes.

After a periodic-pattern submission fails, reject that generator as a whole unless it can be reconciled with every demo. Do not spend further submissions on arbitrary phase or palette variants. Use remaining demos to resolve explicit alternatives (e.g. row-wise versus column-wise sequence, marker-order preservation versus fixed cycle), then validate the candidate on all known examples before resubmitting.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/repeated-pattern-misdirection.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/periodic-phase-validation.md", "content": "# Periodic color-field validation

For patterned full-grid outputs, identify the smallest repeating sequence and its orientation from the entire set of demos. Do not infer a diagonal `(row + column)` phase just because some rows look shifted; test that rule against every cell, including the first row/column, corners, and wraparound. Compare alternatives such as independent row/column cycles or a row-dependent phase.

After an incorrect prediction, discard the complete generator and reconstruct it from all demos. A phase adjustment is only justified if the alternate generator fits every demonstrated cell. Validate the generated test grid's dimensions and cell values before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/periodic-phase-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/mixed-demo-consistency.md", "content": "# Handling apparently inconsistent demonstrations

When one task's demos appear to belong to very different visual families (for example, sparse diagonals versus solid horizontal bands), pause before extrapolating from the newest or most visually obvious pair. Build a concise per-demo record of input structure, output dimensions, palette, and input-to-output mapping. Check whether the underlying transformation is actually invariant across examples, whether the demo contains multiple layers/objects with a hidden correspondence, and whether apparent periodic outputs can be reconstructed from the input rather than guessed.

A periodic output in a single demo is not enough to justify a periodic prediction on the test. Verify the exact cycle, its phase/orientation, and the relation from input markers to output colors using all available pairs. After an incorrect submission, explicitly discard the failed generator and identify what observations falsified it before choosing another action. Do not use `finish` until the environment confirms the puzzle is over; with the demo cap exhausted, continue only with a grounded submission, not an unsupported fallback pattern.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/mixed-demo-consistency.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/trajectory-after-error.md", "content": "# Revising sparse trajectory hypotheses after a wrong output

A wrong submission that extends only one apparent ray is not repaired by shifting its start or changing a single cell. Re-derive the generator from each demo by recording every seed marker, color, direction vector, and endpoint. In the example reviewed here, one demo was identity (a sparse object did not trigger extension), while the larger demo showed simultaneous rays: a 7-ray extended down-right from its marker, and 6-ray(s) extended along the other diagonal direction from multiple marker locations. The test had several 9 markers plus paired 7 and 6 markers; assuming the salient 9-marker cluster was irrelevant and extending just one 6 ray / one short 7 ray was unsupported and was rejected. General lesson: compare the full input geometry to positive and negative demos; infer whether multiple marker colors or every compatible seed launches a ray, and project each ray through the full grid to the evidenced boundary. Preserve original objects and background. Do not submit a one-color or one-ray interpretation unless it accounts for all trajectory behavior in the demonstrations.

This example is not a universal rule for launching rays from all markers: use it to formulate competing hypotheses, then select the one that explains the complete demos and test configuration.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-error-recovery/references/trajectory-after-error.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/ray-inference-failure.md", "content": "# Ray extension inference after failed submissions

When a ray-extension prediction is rejected, reject the entire prior generator instead of making a cosmetic grid edit. Re-derive each ray independently from the demos: identify its seed/anchor coordinates, direction vector, color, whether the seed is part of the ray, and the stopping boundary. Track coexisting rays separately; one demo's ray color or direction must not be generalized to all marker groups without evidence. For a test with multiple potential ray seeds, enumerate candidate rays and validate each against every demo, including unchanged markers and endpoints. If the test has a configuration not represented in demonstrations, spend a demo only when it distinguishes explicitly named alternatives. Validate that generated cells do not overwrite given objects unless outputs prove overwrite behavior.
